INNER JOIN: Definition: Returns rows that have matching values in both tables.

SELECT s.StudentID, s.StudentName, c.CourseName, c.Faculty
FROM Students s
INNER JOIN Courses c
ON s.CourseID = c.CourseID;



Result
StudentID	StudentName	CourseName	Faculty
1		Rahul		DBMS		Prof. A
2		Priya		Java		Prof. B
3		Amit		DBMS		Prof. A
4		Neha		Python		Prof. C



LEFT JOIN: 
Definition: Returns all records from the left table + matched rows from the right table.


SELECT s.StudentID, s.StudentName, c.CourseName, c.Faculty
FROM Students s
LEFT JOIN Courses c
ON s.CourseID = c.CourseID;



Result
StudentID	StudentName	CourseName	Faculty
1		Rahul		DBMS		Prof. A
2		Priya		Java		Prof. B
3		Amit		DBMS		Prof. A
4		Neha		Python		Prof. C
5		Arjun		NULL		NULL




RIGHT JOIN: 
Definition: Returns all records from the right table + matched rows from the left table.

SELECT s.StudentID, s.StudentName, c.CourseName, c.Faculty
FROM Students s
RIGHT JOIN Courses c
ON s.CourseID = c.CourseID;



Result
StudentID	StudentName	CourseName	Faculty
1		Rahul		DBMS		Prof. A
2		Priya		Java		Prof. B
3		Amit		DBMS		Prof. A
4		Neha		Python		Prof. C
NULL		NULL		C++		Prof. D





FULL OUTER JOIN
Definition: Returns all records from both tables, with NULLs where there’s no match.


SELECT s.StudentID, s.StudentName, c.CourseName, c.Faculty
FROM Students s
LEFT JOIN Courses c
ON s.CourseID = c.CourseID
UNION
SELECT s.StudentID, s.StudentName, c.CourseName, c.Faculty
FROM Students s
RIGHT JOIN Courses c
ON s.CourseID = c.CourseID;



Result
StudentID	StudentName	CourseName	Faculty
1		Rahul		DBMS		Prof. A
2		Priya		Java		Prof. B
3		Amit		DBMS		Prof. A
4		Neha		Python		Prof. C
5		Arjun		NULL		NULL
NULL		NULL		C++		Prof. D




CROSS JOIN

Definition: Returns the Cartesian product → every row from Students × every row from Courses.

Query
SELECT s.StudentName, c.CourseName
FROM Students s
CROSS JOIN Courses c;




SELF JOIN

Definition: Join a table with itself (commonly used for hierarchical data).

Example: Students recommending other students

Table: Friends(StudentID, FriendID)

Query
SELECT s.StudentName AS Student, f.StudentName AS Friend
FROM Students s
INNER JOIN Students f
ON s.StudentID = f.CourseID;
